## 2 · Shaft: the integral of α_s·q_c along the pile

#: GEO5 help: F_{max,shaft} = O_{p}·∫ p_{max,shaft} dz, with p_{max,shaft} = α_{s}·q_{c,z} (cap of 15 MPa, far away here: the maximum q_{c} is 1.6 MPa). The integral is done with the trapezoidal rule between readings:
O_p = pi·D_p 'pile perimeter [m]
I_q = dz·(sum(q_c) - (q_c(1) + q_c(n_q))/2) 'integral of q_c along the shaft [MPa·m]
q_med = I_q/L_s 'mean q_c along the shaft [MPa]
R_s = O_p·α_s·I_q·1000 'shaft resistance of one pile [kN]
#hide
r_qm = round(q_med·1000, 1)
r_fm = round(α_s·q_med·1000, 2)
#show
#: The mean q_{c} of the shaft is @{r_qm} kPa: the mean unit friction is only @{r_fm} kPa. These are very soft clays.

## 3 · Base: the three-averages method (Koppejan)

#: GEO5 help (EN 1997-2): p_{max,base} = 0.5·α_{p}·β·s·((q_{c,I,mean} + q_{c,II,mean})/2 + q_{c,III,mean}), with q_{c,I} the average from 0.7·D to 4·D **below** the tip, q_{c,II} the average of the minimum line going up from there to the tip, and q_{c,III} that of the minimum line over the 8·D **above** the tip.
#: **This is NOT reproduced here.** With the readings of the file and that procedure (tried with the zone from 0.7·D to 4·D and with the tip between 13 and 16.6 m) q_{c,I} ≈ 0.48 MPa, q_{c,II} ≈ 0.47 and q_{c,III} ≈ 0.38 come out, and GEO5 prints **0.43, 0.42 and 0.33**: values that the q_{c} below the tip (minimum 0.46 MPa) cannot give as an average. The procedure has to be extracted from the PileCPT_5.dll binary. That is why the base pressure enters as **data from the GEO5 report**:
q_cI = 0.43 'GEO5 average I [MPa]
q_cII = 0.42 'GEO5 average II [MPa]
q_cIII = 0.33 'GEO5 average III [MPa]
p_f = 0.5·α_p·β_f·s_f·((q_cI + q_cII)/2 + q_cIII)·1000 'the formula with the GEO5 rounded averages [kPa]
p_b = 374.12 'maximum pressure at the tip, from the GEO5 report [kPa]
#: The formula with the averages rounded to 2 decimals gives 377.5 kPa; GEO5 uses the unrounded averages and gives 374.12.
R_b = p_b·A_b 'base resistance of one pile [kN]

## 4 · One pile and the group (EN 1997-2, as the example)

